  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1110 AM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nDry conditions are expected through tonight, although a few\nlight showers possible across portions of the Frank Church and\nnorth of Banner Summit. Light precipitation will be very slow \nto progress east until tomorrow evening, with the best chance \nuntil that point north and west of Galena Summit to Leadore. The\nstorm slowly works across eastern Idaho Tuesday and Tuesday\nnight. Although this is another split flow system, it appears\nthe main part of this low will actually swing closer to eastern\nIdaho vs. the last one and pull away to the southeast. We are\nseeing just a slight increase in precipitation totals over the\npast 24 hours. Favored areas of precipitation are still across\nthe Sawtooths and surrounding ranges, the eastern highlands\n(including Island Park), and the South Hills/Albion Mountains\nthrough the Bear River Range. Amounts up to 0.50\" are currently\nin the forecast from WPC, with some locally higher amounts\nalong the Montana border and into portions of Lemhi County.\nOther mountain ranges are generally looking at 0.35\" or less.\nFor most lower valleys look for a few hundredths to around\n0.20-0.25\". Higher end amounts in the valleys, central \nmountains, and eastern highlands are due to stronger upslope \nconditions. In terms of snowfall, most snowfall accumulations\nwill be above 6500ft although some light accumulations may occur\nbetween 5000-6000ft...especially north of Idaho Falls and \nIsland Park area. We would not be surprised to see a mix of rain\nand snow or wet snow falling on the back side of the storm and\ncold front, although accumulations are not forecast at the\nmoment. Snowfall is generally 1-4\" across the mountains, with a\nfew spot seeing 4-8\". Most of those spots are above 9000ft,\nalthough Galena and Banner Summits are slated to see 4-5.5\"\npotentially. With this being over 48 hours and even with \nlooking at periods of heavier snowfall, we are not seeing enough\nimpacts to travel and such for any WINTER WEATHER ADVISORY\nheadlines for now. The concern again is that some of the higher\nresolution models/ensembles and the AI versions of models\nproducing 0.20-0.40\" more precipitation, if not more on higher\nridgelines. Given the pattern, those types of numbers are not\nvery plausible and/or have any type of confidence to boost the\nforecast. It will be interesting to see where models trend in\nthe next 24 hours. The models continue to show a rather chaotic\npattern with multiple storms moving through the western U.S. The\nconcern is that split flow continues to dominate the pattern, so\ntrying to pinpoint more precipitation with any one of those\nstorms is pretty difficult. If any trend exists, it is toward\ncooler temperatures...which would mean a better potential for\nsnow or a mix at lower elevations.",
